// Bridge troll, step 3 of 6: a loop that changes what its guard reads, deferred so it reads the change.
//
// Teaches: recursion with progress. The designers make the trunk break after three slams, so `attack_enemy` gets a
// `rearm` method: walk to a tree, uproot it, then attack again. The source plans the self-call at once, because its
// planner applies UprootTrunk's effect (trunk health = 3) while planning. Ours does not simulate effects: a direct
// `(attack_enemy ?inp_enemy)` here would still read trunk health 0 and pick `rearm` again at every level until the
// planner gives up with `depth_exceeded` (test/examples/canon/bridge-troll.test.ts shows it). So the self-call is
// deferred, `&attack_enemy`: it is decomposed when the runner reaches it, after !uproot_trunk ran and wrote the new
// trunk. The rule: every recursive step changes what its guard reads, and without effect simulation the step after
// the change must be a `&` task (or the plan ends and the root replans).
//
// Changed from v2: `holding_trunk` became `trunk_health ?hits` (slams left; 0: no trunk); new method `rearm` with
//   the deferred self-call; new fact `trunk_nearby`; new primitive !uproot_trunk.
// Source: Troy Humphreys, "Exploring HTN Planners through Example", Game AI Pro (CRC Press, 2013), chapter 12,
//   section 12.6:
//   https://www.gameaipro.com/GameAIPro/GameAIPro_Chapter12_Exploring_HTN_Planners_through_Example.pdf
//   and bevy_htn `troll.htn` (AttackEnemy's "Trunk depleted" method, whose comment already asks whether to "pause
//   execution of plan here instead of recursing"): https://github.com/RJ/bevy_htn/blob/422c8614ec1cc4d7f0382d93a835bcc6eab36d80/bevy_htn/assets/troll.htn
//   (c) Richard Jones; adapted (renamed, re-shaped).
// Licence: the chapter is (c) CRC Press, re-expressed (nothing copied); bevy_htn is MIT OR Apache-2.0, adapted here
//   under MIT; its notice is in ../THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md.
// Deviations from the sources:
//   - deferred self-call `&attack_enemy` instead of planning through simulated effects (see above);
//   - FindTrunk (an operator that picks the tree) became the host sensor `trunk_nearby`, so `rearm` has a condition;
//   - the boulder method of v2 stays as the last method, for a troll with no trunk and no tree in reach (the
//     chapter adds it back in section 12.8 for "could not navigate to the enemy").
// Not ported: nothing else of section 12.6.
// Next: v4_last_seen.domain chases an enemy that slipped out of sight.
//
// Facts (host sensors): (enemy_visible ?enemy) (next_bridge ?bridge) as before; (trunk_health ?hits) int, written by
//   !trunk_slam (-1) and !uproot_trunk (3); (trunk_nearby ?tree) uprootable trees, nearest first.
// Primitives (logged): (!navigate_to ?target) (!trunk_slam ?enemy) (!uproot_trunk ?tree) (!lift_boulder)
//   (!throw_boulder ?enemy) (!check_bridge ?bridge) (!idle ?seconds)
// Scenarios: worlds/v3_rearm/; the rearm story: stories.ts; npm run htn:run -- --world v3_rearm/2_rearm_at_the_oak

(:domain BridgeTrollV3 top_level_domain

    (:method (be_troll) top_level_method

        (fight
            (and
                (enemy_visible ?enemy)
            )
            (
                (attack_enemy ?enemy)
            )
        )

        (patrol
            (and
                (next_bridge ?bridge)
            )
            (
                (!navigate_to ?bridge)
                (!check_bridge ?bridge)
            )
        )

        (idle
            ()
            (
                (!idle 2.0)
            )
        )
    )

    // Hurt the enemy. Pre: ?inp_enemy is in sight. Effect: one attack made, or a new trunk and then an attack.
    (:method (attack_enemy ?inp_enemy)

        (trunk_slam
            (and
                (trunk_health ?hits)
                (> ?hits 0)
            )
            (
                (!navigate_to ?inp_enemy)
                (!trunk_slam ?inp_enemy)
            )
        )

        // The trunk is spent: fetch a new one, then decide again against the world after the uproot.
        (rearm
            (and
                (trunk_nearby ?tree)
            )
            (
                (!navigate_to ?tree)
                (!uproot_trunk ?tree)
                (&attack_enemy ?inp_enemy)
            )
        )

        (throw_boulder
            ()
            (
                (!lift_boulder)
                (!throw_boulder ?inp_enemy)
            )
        )
    )
)
